\begin{abstract}
The rapid expansion of edge-computing platforms has created a pressing need for energy-efficient neural network inference, as existing quantization and compression techniques compromise model accuracy or demand extensive hardware support, limiting their deployment on resource-constrained devices. We introduce a mixed-precision quantization framework that jointly optimizes layer-wise bit-widths and hardware-aware scaling factors via a differentiable search, representing a novel synergy between automated search and hardware-aware optimization. This approach enables the adaptation of precision settings to meet strict latency budgets while preserving model accuracy. Experiments demonstrate significant reductions in inference energy with minimal accuracy loss, enabling the deployment of state-of-the-art vision models on micro-controllers and opening new opportunities for on-device AI in real-world applications, with up to 3.2× reduction in inference energy and less than 1% top-1 accuracy loss compared to full-precision baselines.
\end{abstract}